      "text": "To solve for \\(\\sin A\\) in the right triangle \\(ABC\\) with \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), we first need to identify the side lengths and use the trigonometric definition for sine in a right triangle. \n\nGiven:\n- \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), which means \\(A\\) is the right angle.\n- \\(AB = 16\\)\n- \\(BC = 24\\)\n\nIn a right triangle, the sine of an angle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the hypotenuse. \n\nSince \\(\\angle A\\) is the right angle, opposite angles are not present; a sine of 90 degrees is always \\(1\\). Thus, \\(\\sin 90^\\circ = 1\\). However, the given specific notation of \\(\\sin A\\) in terms of opposite side \\(A\\) and hypotenuse \\(C\\) in a right triangle should be \\(\\sin C\\) because the angle \\(C\\) opposite side \\(A\\) should be \\(45^\\circ\\), therefore,\n\n\\(\\sin C = \\sin 45^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\).\n\nTherefore according to the choice provided correctly,\\(\\sin A = \\boxed{\\sin 45^\\circ} = \\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}\\).",
