      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": " \nTo find \\(\\sin A\\), we first need to determine the length of the side opposite to angle \\(A\\), which is side \\(BC\\). Given that \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), angle \\(A\\) is a right angle. Therefore, in the right triangle \\(ABC\\), side \\(BC\\) is the hypotenuse and side \\(AB\\) is the side adjacent to angle \\(A\\).\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of side \\(AC\\), which is the side opposite to angle \\(A\\). The Pythagorean theorem states that:\n\n\\[\nAB^2 + AC^2 = BC^2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\n\\[\n16^2 + AC^2 = 24^2\n\\]\n\nCalculating the squares:\n\n\\[\n256 + AC^2 = 576\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(AC^2\\):\n\n\\[\nAC^2 = 576 - 256\n\\]\n\n\\[\nAC^2 = 320\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides:\n\n\\[\nAC = \\sqrt{320}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\nAC = \\sqrt{64 \\times 5} = 8\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nNow, we know that \\(\\sin A\\) is given by the ratio of the length of the side opposite to angle \\(A\\) to the hypotenuse. Therefore:\n\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{AC}{BC} = \\frac{8\\sqrt{5}}{24}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the fraction:\n\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{6} = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}}\n\\]",
