      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find \\(\\sin A\\) in the right triangle \\(ABC\\) where \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), we need to understand that in any right triangle, the sine of one of the non-right angles is equivalent to the ratio of the length of the side opposite that angle to the length of the hypotenuse. Given that \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), the side opposite to \\(\\angle A\\) must be the hypotenuse, and the hypotenuse is clearly \\(BC\\).\n\nThe vertices of the triangle are denoted as follows:\n- \\(A\\) at the right angle,\n- \\(B\\) at the bottom left,\n- \\(C\\) at the bottom right.\n\nIn this right triangle, the hypotenuse \\(BC\\) has length 24, and the side opposite to \\(\\angle A\\) (which is the same as \\(\\angle A\\) because \\(\\angle A\\) is the right angle) is the leg \\(AB\\) with length 16.\n\nThus, \\(\\sin A\\) is defined as the ratio of the length of the side opposite \\(\\angle A\\) to the hypotenuse. Since the side opposite to \\(\\angle A\\) is the leg \\(AB\\) and the hypotenuse is the hypotenuse \\(BC\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{AB}{BC} = \\frac{16}{24}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the ratio:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{16}{24} = \\frac{16 \\div 8}{24 \\div 8} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nHence, the value of \\(\\sin A\\) is \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]",
